{"context":"Boundaries\n\n| Domain | Owns | Must not depend on |\n|---|---|---|\n| Project | Repository, `anvil.yaml`, source validation, build/package workflow | Runtime Registry, Runtime State |\n| Artifact | Immutable payload, `manifest.json`, Artifact identity and verification | Runtime filesystem and Release state |\n| Deployment | Targets, transport, authentication, orchestration, compatibility negotiation | Runtime Registry and Runtime internal layout |\n| Server Runtime | Registry, State, Artifact installation, Release lifecycle, activation, rollback, locks, health | Repository, CI vendor, transport implementation | Source: ADR-015-four-domain-architecture-and-deployment-context.md Date:2026-07-19 Status:Accepted.","decision":"Anvil is organized into four bounded contexts with one-way dependency flow:\n\n```text\nProject → Artifact → Deployment → Server Runtime\n```\n\nThe Project Domain is repository-aware and produces Artifacts. The Artifact Domain owns immutable payloads and the embedded `manifest.json` contract. The Deployment Domain transports and orchestrates delivery without reading Runtime internals. The Server Runtime Domain installs Artifacts, creates and manages Runtime Releases, and owns activation, rollback, Registry, State, locking, shared resources, and health.","consequences":"- A new Deployment bounded context exists even when its implementation is initially minimal.\n- Runtime documentation must not describe CI, transport, or repository behavior as Runtime responsibilities.\n- Project and Deployment documentation must not derive data from the Runtime Registry.\n- Artifact and Release terminology must remain distinct.\n- Future work requiring communication between contexts must define an explicit contract instead of introducing reverse dependencies.","alternativesConsidered":"Alternatives see source doc."}